WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:04.640
Batman is finally living up to the&nbsp;
"detective" part of "detective comics."&nbsp;

00:00:04.640 --> 00:00:09.120
In the new movie "The Batman," directed by Matt&nbsp;
Reeves and starring Robert Pattinson as the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.120 --> 00:00:14.800
"Caped Crusader," fans of the classic DC character&nbsp;
are introduced to a new version of Bruce Wayne.&nbsp;

00:00:14.800 --> 00:00:18.560
The film opens with a brutal murder,&nbsp;
revealing that there is a new serial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.560 --> 00:00:24.160
killer on the rampage in Gotham City, aiming&nbsp;
to expose the lies of corrupt public officials.&nbsp;

00:00:24.160 --> 00:00:30.240
Meanwhile, a thriving illegal business circulating&nbsp;
drugs called "Drops" is run by Carmine Falcone and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.240 --> 00:00:35.280
Oswald Cobblepot, a.k.a. the Penguin, and&nbsp;
Bruce is caught in the middle of it all,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.280 --> 00:00:39.600
trying to help save his city and the people in it.
A lot of the characters in the movie are very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.600 --> 00:00:44.720
familiar to fans, including Selina&nbsp;
Kyle, a.k.a. Catwoman, James Gordon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.720 --> 00:00:48.480
and Alfred Pennyworth. But there&nbsp;
are also some striking changes in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.480 --> 00:00:52.800
"The Batman" compared to other screen&nbsp;
adaptations of the superhero's story.&nbsp;

00:00:52.800 --> 00:00:57.680
In this case, the biggest change comes in the&nbsp;
form of Batman's goals and everyday behavior.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.680 --> 00:01:02.400
In "The Batman," Bruce truly takes on the role&nbsp;
of detective, looking closely at all of the clues&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.400 --> 00:01:08.800
laid out by the Riddler in order to catch him.
"You came; I've been trying to reach you."&nbsp;

00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:13.920
In "The Batman," the defining difference between&nbsp;
this version of Bruce Wayne and any other we've&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.920 --> 00:01:18.720
seen on the big screen is that Pattinson's&nbsp;
Batman is more detective-like than ever before.&nbsp;

00:01:18.720 --> 00:01:23.120
Although he's still a vigilante who uses&nbsp;
technology and his fists to beat up the expected&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.120 --> 00:01:28.720
criminals on the street, he also understands that&nbsp;
careful observation and investigation can get him&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.720 --> 00:01:33.760
far, especially when it comes to the Riddler.
The Riddler is the main antagonist of the film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.760 --> 00:01:38.640
and he is introduced through the murder of Mayor&nbsp;
Don Mitchell Jr. Bruce shows up alongside the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.640 --> 00:01:43.440
police officers and detectives of Gotham City&nbsp;
to the crime scene, called there by Gordon.&nbsp;

00:01:43.440 --> 00:01:47.840
While one main reason Gordon calls him there&nbsp;
is likely the letter addressed "to the Batman"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.840 --> 00:01:52.960
left by the body, it's probably also due to&nbsp;
Bruce's talent in taking notice of the details&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.960 --> 00:01:57.600
and tracking down criminals. For example, Bruce&nbsp;
quickly takes note of a key piece of evidence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.600 --> 00:02:02.720
nearby: the blood spatter on the ground from the&nbsp;
carpeting tool Riddler uses as the murder weapon.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.720 --> 00:02:06.800
At this point, it looks like the investigative&nbsp;
team hasn't even spotted it yet themselves,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.800 --> 00:02:12.000
as a crime scene photographer notes his actions&nbsp;
and photographs the blood after Bruce moves away.&nbsp;

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:16.960
Bruce brings Gordon along early on to the mayor's&nbsp;
car, where he finds the USB drive that the Riddler&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.960 --> 00:02:21.600
left containing evidence of his shady dealings at&nbsp;
the club. He also finds the note in the Riddler's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.600 --> 00:02:26.480
apartment hidden in the bat cage all on his own.&nbsp;
Overall, Bruce clearly understands the value of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.480 --> 00:02:30.640
intelligence and patience, particularly&nbsp;
getting into the detective mindset as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.640 --> 00:02:34.960
he tracks down the Riddler, who challenges&nbsp;
him through the constant riddles as clues.&nbsp;

00:02:34.960 --> 00:02:38.960
Seriously, Bruce is surprisingly skilled&nbsp;
at riddles, showcasing Reeves' decision&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.960 --> 00:02:42.960
to emphasize Bruce's mental strength&nbsp;
alongside his physical capabilities.&nbsp;

00:02:43.520 --> 00:02:47.600
"Justice. The answer's 'justice.'"
Although Bruce spends a lot of "The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.600 --> 00:02:52.400
Batman" fighting criminals on the street, he is&nbsp;
actively investigating the crimes of the Riddler,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.400 --> 00:02:56.560
Cobblepot, Falcone, and the corrupt&nbsp;
officials Riddler brings to light.&nbsp;

00:02:56.560 --> 00:03:01.680
In this investigation, Gordon is arguably his&nbsp;
right hand man, and the police detective clearly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.680 --> 00:03:07.200
respects Bruce's abilities and drive for justice.&nbsp;
At one point in the film, he even says that Bruce&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.200 --> 00:03:11.520
is the only one he trusts, representing&nbsp;
his unwavering loyalty to the vigilante&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.520 --> 00:03:15.440
whose real identity he doesn't even know.
The two connect through the bat signal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.440 --> 00:03:19.040
when Bruce needs to go out and fight&nbsp;
criminals on a particularly rough night,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.040 --> 00:03:23.760
but also when they have some important information&nbsp;
to share with one another. Usually, Bruce provides&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.760 --> 00:03:28.160
Gordon with all the new information he finds --&nbsp;
though he kind of jumps the gun in the case of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.160 --> 00:03:33.840
Riddler, even enacting an undercover scheme with&nbsp;
Selina at the club without telling Gordon, likely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.840 --> 00:03:38.240
because it's more personal than other cases.
Gordon in "The Batman" is not too different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.240 --> 00:03:42.160
from other iterations of the character&nbsp;
in that he values truth and justice over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.160 --> 00:03:46.000
violence and vengeance -- though there&nbsp;
does seem to be some leeway for him here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.000 --> 00:03:50.240
as he looks the other way a bit, allowing Batman&nbsp;
to rough up some criminals here and there.&nbsp;

00:03:50.240 --> 00:03:54.560
But all in all, it's Bruce's just nature that&nbsp;
convinces Gordon to keep working with him on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.560 --> 00:03:59.840
cases, and considering he literally brings the&nbsp;
vigilante to a crime scene, he clearly values&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.840 --> 00:04:04.880
Bruce's detective abilities – unlike some people.
"How am I a part of this?"&nbsp;

00:04:06.720 --> 00:04:10.000
"Oh, you're really not as&nbsp;
smart as I thought you were."&nbsp;

